### Comparison with Logseq (Org mode)

While Logseq can read and write `.org` files, its internal data model differs significantly from native Emacs Org-mode and Org-roam:

| Feature | Org-roam | Logseq (`.org`) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Model** | **Document-first:** Arbitrary prose, paragraphs, and headings. | **Outliner-first:** Almost all content is written as nested bullet points (`- `). |
| **Page Links** | By ID: `[[id:<uuid>][Page Title]]` | By Title: `[[Page Name]]` or `#tag` |
| **Block Links** | Target ID in heading drawer: `[[id:<uuid>][Heading]]` | UUID in double parens: `((uuid))` |
| **Properties** | Standard Org drawers: `:PROPERTIES: ... :END:` | Logseq `key:: value` pairs (either page-level at top, or inline below bullets). |
| **Node Granularity** | Files and headings explicitly tagged with an `:ID:`. | Every single bullet item is an addressable block. |
| **Database** | Emacsql (SQLite) parses and syncs Org files in the background. | Datalog (in-memory) maintains the graph database. |

### Practical Interoperability

* **Org-roam $\to$ Logseq:** Logseq will parse file titles, but body text not nested under bullets (`- `) is treated as a single monolith block. Org-roam `id:` links won't resolve as native page references in Logseq.
* **Logseq $\to$ Org-roam:** Logseq files look like deeply nested lists in Emacs. Without `:ID:` properties, Org-roam will not recognize Logseq pages or blocks as nodes until you generate IDs for them.

